    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "512ab3ee_5c5120c6",
        "filename": "runtime/oat/sdc_file.cc",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 55,
      "author": {
        "id": 1884045
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-03-18T12:05:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m basically following [what the runtime does](https://cs.android.com/android/platform/superproject/main/+/main:art/runtime/gc/space/image_space.cc;l\u003d3427;drc\u003dd7acd7d5623f67aa959ae23a362c337288e96d69) when loading the OAT file. It doesn\u0027t looks like the runtime is vetting the value. AFAIK, this value can even be an empty string, in testing environments, so there isn\u0027t much we can verify here.",
      "parentUuid": "83dfcd5e_d8c8e74f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 55,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 55,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "ac0c568e04389babe94db101353cf829b617bf69",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "723281a9_989c07df",
        "filename": "runtime/oat/sdc_file.cc",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 55,
      "author": {
        "id": 1373864
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-03-19T13:53:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not convinced by justifying one weakness with another, sorry. ;)\n\nWhat I\u0027m thinking of is checking with a regexp, something like `[0-9./]*` (or use something simpler like `strspn`). There are several reasons why that\u0027s useful - one is that it puts to rest any worry that this can be used as a vector to inject gadgets.",
      "parentUuid": "512ab3ee_5c5120c6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 55,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 55,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "ac0c568e04389babe94db101353cf829b617bf69",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6ee05a0a_136a15a5",
        "filename": "runtime/oat/sdc_file.cc",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 55,
      "author": {
        "id": 1884045
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-03-19T15:13:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I can\u0027t say I agree.\n\nIMO, SDC is just a file format to persist the apex versions string produced and consumed by the runtime, so it shouldn\u0027t have much opinion on how the string should look like. If the string is in the wrong format, then it\u0027s the runtime\u0027s responsibility to reject it (by the code [here](https://android-review.git.corp.google.com/c/platform/art/+/3539980/7/runtime/gc/space/image_space.cc)). In fact, the runtime does a string match, so a string in the wrong format does get rejected, making a check here look redundant.\n\nMoreover, this is irrelevant to security. SDC files are only in the `oat` and `dalvik-cache` directories, with SELinux label `dalvikcache_data_file`, only writable by ART, meaning if an attacker can use SDC to hack the runtime, then they can already use ODEX files to do so, so this doesn\u0027t introduce a attack vector.\n\nAnyway, I don\u0027t feel my opinion on this is particularly strong, and your proposed check lookd lenient enough against potential future changes, so I added it.",
      "parentUuid": "723281a9_989c07df",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 55,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 55,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "ac0c568e04389babe94db101353cf829b617bf69",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4a5cdb2f_f79feb4d",
        "filename": "runtime/oat/sdc_file.cc",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 55,
      "author": {
        "id": 1373864
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-03-19T19:05:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thank you.\n\nIt\u0027s a matter of hygiene and division of concerns - just handle all the parsing when the file is read, and avoid long arguments about why it\u0027s fine anyway, in particular if they depend on the current state of the code in other parts of the code base and hence are fragile.",
      "parentUuid": "6ee05a0a_136a15a5",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 55,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 55,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "ac0c568e04389babe94db101353cf829b617bf69",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7a89598e_e3e63748",
        "filename": "runtime/oat/sdc_file.cc",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 55,
      "author": {
        "id": 1884045
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-03-20T11:23:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s indeed a matter of division of concerns, and there where our opinions differ. I actually think the opposite, which is that let\u0027s handle all possible inputs in the runtime\u0027s verification logic, to avoid assumptions on how the inputs get sanitized before reaching the runtime, because I think such assumptions are also fragile. I can\u0027t say one way of division is obviously better than the other.\n\nAnyway, either way is fine.",
      "parentUuid": "4a5cdb2f_f79feb4d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 55,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 55,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "ac0c568e04389babe94db101353cf829b617bf69",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
